From a chat (a phone, a session with the plugin and no command typed), the operator runs this lifecycle through the plugin's operator skills, the chat entry points: `buildermesh:ideate` ("proposals for X", "run a round on X") runs `ideate` on the row's `host` (locally, else over ssh), with whatever the operator asked beyond the repo's name as `--context`, then watches the round with `bmesh watch --nodes <repo>-round-<N>`, relays an engineer's `ask`, and tells the operator in the chat when the round is proposed (its ranked pitch) or blocked, and, when the operator names one specific idea of their own instead (decided by what was asked, not the words), starts no two-thread round: it runs `ideate --idea` (a round of one engineer thread, on the row's `host`) when the operator asks for a thread, a model or an effort, or the idea needs the repo read and measured at length, and otherwise writes that idea's spec in the session (one half-pager, or two or three in build order for an idea too big for one lead, `proposed_by: operator`) and lands it on `main` as `proposed`; `buildermesh:choose-ideas` (the reply to a pitch in plain words) runs `choose-ideas`; `buildermesh:build` ("build X 3", "build what's accepted in X") runs `build` on the row's `host` (a `held` refusal it waits out with `bmesh watch` on the named nodes, draining its own family first, and runs again; one held by another coordinator's family, or a red `main`, it reports to the operator), then watches the leads with `bmesh watch --nodes <paths>` and relays each `ask` to the operator, answering it with `bmesh --node operator send`, holds and releases leads with `hold` and `unhold` instead of a free-text hold ("hold X", "hold land on X", "release X"; see Holds) and relays a lead reaching its gate as one line, and once a family's watch is done hands the issues naming bmesh's own repo to `bmesh fix` (see The fixer) and turns the rest into accepted and proposed specs and drains them (see Feedback); `buildermesh:scaffold` ("scaffold X") runs `scaffold --check`, shows the diff, runs `scaffold`, the row's verify, commits `Scaffold the shared definition of done and proposals` and pushes; `buildermesh:mesh-status` ("what's building", "where is X") summarises `mesh-status` (and `spec list` for a named repo) as working, asking, silent and done since the last look. `propose` and `build` carry the Fable fallback the binary has not: a Fable model the runner rejects is run once more with `--model claude-opus-5-5`, telling the operator what the runner said. Each skill ends with one line: what is started or landed, and what waits on the operator, naming each thing by what it is in plain words (a spec or issue by what it does, its number after it in brackets if at all), so the line reads on its own; the done rule `scaffold` writes and every build brief's closing end the same way (`brief.PlainWords`).
